## Prompt 1

> /hackathon-firstprot read the context from README.md. Write schemas.py
> defining the inter-stage data contracts for this pipeline as pydantic
> models (for the JSON manifest) and pandera DataFrameSchemas (for the
> tabular artifacts). Every later stage must validate its inputs on read and
> its outputs on write against these schemas — prose comments describing
> column names are not sufficient and have repeatedly failed on projects of
> this shape. Define at minimum: GenesSchema, GeneSetsSchema,
> InteractionsSchema, OTAssociationsSchema, Manifest [full field lists as
> specified in README.md/PROMPTS.md Stage 0]. Note in the module docstring
> that source_db is retained as a column even though only reactome is
> emitted in this version... Do not add KEGG, WikiPathways or OmniPath
> support now. Use code-improvement-advisor for code reviewing and
> improvement.

**Changes:**
- Read `README.md` and `PROMPTS.md` (Stage 0 spec) for the exact schema requirements.
- Created `schemas.py`: pandera `DataFrameSchema`s for `GenesSchema`, `GeneSetsSchema`,
  `InteractionsSchema`, `OTAssociationsSchema` (Ensembl gene-ID regex on every gene
  column, `source_db`/`sign`/`evidence_type` vocab checks, `score`/`confidence` range
  checks, composite `unique=["set_id","gene_id"]`), plus pydantic v2 models for the
  `Manifest` (nested `ResolvedTarget`, `ResolvedDisease`, `Source`, `SourceFile`,
  `OutputArtifact`, `CoverageEntry`, `ScaleReport`) and an `assert_foreign_key` helper
  for cross-table FK checks pandera can't express. Module docstring documents the
  `source_db`/future-adapter-seam decision as instructed.
- Created `test_schemas.py` (pytest) asserting each schema rejects malformed input
  (missing column, wrong dtype, out-of-vocabulary value, HGNC-symbol-as-gene_id — the
  real v1 `GRB2-1` bug class) as well as accepting valid fixtures.
- Added `pandas` to `requirements.txt`'s Stage 0 block (needed directly by
  `schemas.py`/`test_schemas.py`, not just transitively via pandera); `pytest` was
  already listed under Testing.
- Caught and fixed a real bug during self-testing: schema-wide `coerce=True` was
  silently coercing a garbage-string `directed` column to `True` instead of raising.
  Rescoped `coerce=True` to only the three numeric nullable columns that legitimately
  need it (`hierarchy_level`, `confidence`, `score`); everything else stayed strict.
- Ran the code-improvement-advisor agent on both files. It found a narrower residual
  instance of the same coercion trap (bool→float silently passing `confidence`/`score`
  range checks) and, judging a fix too complex for a case that can't occur with current
  data sources, documented it in a comment and pinned the current behavior with a test
  instead. It also added cross-field consistency validators to `CoverageEntry`
  (`percent_mapped` and `dropped_count` must agree with `raw_gene_count`/`mapped_count`)
  and ~25 additional tests (strict-mode extra-column rejection per schema,
  `assert_foreign_key` coverage, coercion-path tests, empty-dataframe acceptance).
- Verified: `python3 -m pytest test_schemas.py -v` → 42 passed.

